  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of the tripartite constituents to design and implement policies to promote gender equality and tackle discrimination, and violence and harassment</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Violence and harassment in the workplace remain underreported in Albania, despite evidence of their prevalence. UN reports indicate that 52.9% of women have experienced violence in their lifetime. A 2019 study found that 43% of employees identified unwelcome touching and physical harassment as common, while 40% reported verbal harassment. Workplace violence was most frequently reported between employers and employees (49% of women, 41% of men), followed by incidents among colleagues (26%). Additionally, 23% of respondents recognized workplace violence as a widespread national issue. Albania took a significant step in addressing this problem by ratifying ILO Convention No. 190 on February 3, 2022. In 2024-2025, in collaboration with the Independent Trade Unions of Albania (BSPSH) and the Albanian Confederation of Trade Unions (KSSH), awareness and training initiatives are underway in eight municipalities: Elbasan, Gramsh, Tepelena, Lushnja, and Divjaka (covered by BSPSH) and Vlora, Erseka, and Mirdita (covered by KSSH). The initiative aims to develop and implement workplace policies that protect workers in these municipalities and those serving in the social service institutions established by these municipalities.</narrative>
  </description>
